WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.795
Integer conversions can lose, reinterpret,

2
00:00:02.875 --> 00:00:07.223
or misrepresent data when the destination
type cannot represent the original value.

3
00:00:07.303 --> 00:00:11.168
That risk appears when parsing input,
storing values into narrower fields,

4
00:00:11.248 --> 00:00:14.569
calling platform APIs, serializing
records, reading metadata,

5
00:00:14.649 --> 00:00:17.307
or moving values between signed and
unsigned types.

6
00:00:17.387 --> 00:00:21.674
A narrowing conversion can discard
high-order information and leave a value that no

7
00:00:21.754 --> 00:00:23.928
longer represents the original quantity.

8
00:00:24.008 --> 00:00:25.820
If the program parsed a large count,

9
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:28.376
then stores it in a smaller type for
convenience,

10
00:00:28.456 --> 00:00:32.684
later checks may operate on the converted
value rather than the value that actually

11
00:00:32.764 --> 00:00:33.247
arrived.

12
00:00:33.327 --> 00:00:35.682
That creates a gap between validation and
use.

13
00:00:35.762 --> 00:00:40.170
Signed-to-unsigned conversion can turn a
negative value into a large unsigned value.

14
00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:43.934
This is especially important when a value
later controls a size, offset,

15
00:00:44.014 --> 00:00:45.403
allocation, or loop bound.

16
00:00:45.483 --> 00:00:47.295
If negative values are not allowed,

17
00:00:47.375 --> 00:00:51.663
reject them before conversion rather than
hoping the destination type will make the

18
00:00:51.743 --> 00:00:52.769
problem disappear.

19
00:00:52.849 --> 00:00:55.748
Unsigned-to-signed conversion deserves the
same care.

20
00:00:55.828 --> 00:01:00.116
A value may be outside the intended
business range or outside the destination type's

21
00:01:00.196 --> 00:01:01.343
representable range.

22
00:01:01.423 --> 00:01:03.175
Depending on the type and context,

23
00:01:03.255 --> 00:01:05.791
the result may not be what the developer
intended.

24
00:01:05.871 --> 00:01:10.098
The safe design is to check
representability and business meaning before storing.

25
00:01:10.178 --> 00:01:11.688
Casts do not validate values.

26
00:01:11.768 --> 00:01:13.278
A cast requests a conversion;

27
00:01:13.358 --> 00:01:16.679
it does not prove that the source value
fits the destination type,

28
00:01:16.759 --> 00:01:18.692
that the destination unit is correct,

29
00:01:18.772 --> 00:01:21.369
or that the value is appropriate for the
operation.

30
00:01:21.449 --> 00:01:25.495
A cast that silences a warning without a
nearby range check should be treated as a

31
00:01:25.575 --> 00:01:26.239
review item.

32
00:01:26.319 --> 00:01:29.943
API boundaries are common conversion risk
points: parsing functions,

33
00:01:30.023 --> 00:01:33.284
file formats, network formats, database
fields, system calls,

34
00:01:33.364 --> 00:01:37.168
library callbacks, serialization, and
callbacks from lower-level code.

35
00:01:37.248 --> 00:01:41.536
Store values only after validating that
the destination type and business range can

36
00:01:41.616 --> 00:01:42.462
represent them.

37
00:01:42.542 --> 00:01:45.380
That validation should be visible to the
next reviewer.
